# Emergency banner

Use an urgent banner only for a verified, current condition that needs attention now. A missing or old reading must never look like an all-clear. Government event categories do not transfer to a household dashboard, so this page keeps only the structure.

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation. They do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## Keep urgency separate from presentation

Use the [message decision guide](../../components/status.html#choose-the-right-message) before choosing this variant. It sits inside the broader [banner family](../../components/notification-banners.html) with its own urgency and freshness requirement.

## When to use it

Reserve an urgent household message for a condition such as a smoke alarm reporting smoke. Say what happened, where, when it was reported and what to do next. Keep routine maintenance, expiry reminders and a disconnected non-critical sensor in ordinary status components.

## Structure and behaviour

Name the condition in text and keep the integration timestamp and availability. Announce a new urgent alert once. Leave an alert that is already present readable without interrupting assistive technology again. Do not use colour alone to show severity.

## Home Assistant boundary

The theme does not decide that something is an emergency, act as an alarm system or replace independent warning equipment. The examples are static specimens labelled as examples. No simulated emergency is injected into Home Assistant, and no government emergency branding is reused.

